{"author":{"id":"f943aa1c5ee078b881a8e97535a4e2ccb401d01ca00f4128126befa9cca58a32"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792407949,"nonce":"/0p6v5CsLmGLn3q+D7WqRQj5jTc=","title":"Releases: four decisions to discuss (APP_VERSION, one-hour grace, refusals before tagging, provenance)","message":"These calls were made while building the release pipeline (`docs/prd/release.md`, `docs/prd/release-plan.md`). Tasks 1–14 are on main. Each section below gives what was decided, why, what it costs, and the question to settle.\n\n\n**Decided.** On every deploy, the overlay's `version_values` (co2: `backend.env.APP_VERSION`) are written with the deployed version. The chart entry's `version` is written only when the deploy packaged a chart, or when the release record holds one. A record without a chart keeps the overlay's current chart version. Two kinds of record lack a chart:\n- a release from before git-board;\n- the record Reconcile writes for an interrupted cut.\n\n**Why.** APP_VERSION is what the running app reports, so it must match what runs. Packaging an old commit's chart at the release version would ship a chart that never ran.\n\n**Cost.** After an interrupted cut, that release runs the previous chart version. Its images and APP_VERSION are right.\n\n**To settle:**\n- Should co2's chart be versioned on its own, bumped only when `helm/` changes, instead of riding the app version?\n- `version_values` live under the chart entry's `valuesInline`, so an overlay with no chart can't carry APP_VERSION. Should they also be writable without a chart?\n\n\n**Decided.** Any clone runs Reconcile at the start of cut, promote, rollback and deploy. It writes a record for a record-less tag only once the tag is **more than an hour old**. Older tags are recorded with whatever images are found, even none.\n\n**Why.** A cut runs in this order:\n1. it pushes its tag first: the compare-and-swap on the version name;\n2. it builds the images, which takes minutes;\n3. it writes the chart;\n4. it writes the record.\n\nWithout a grace, another clone's Reconcile in the middle of a cut writes a partial record, with no chart, no notes, and possibly missing images. Records are one per commit and the earliest wins, so the partial record could be the one people see.\n\n**Cost.** It's a heuristic:\n- a cut that takes more than an hour between its tag and its record still races;\n- a tag cut outside git-board shows no record for an hour;\n- an interrupted cut's record appears up to an hour late.\n\n**Options:**\n- **a.** A lease written by the cut (a \"cutting\" ref or op with an expiry). Reconcile skips leased tags.\n- **b.** Write the record at tag time and add artifacts later. Records are frozen today, so this needs an \"add artifacts\" op.\n- **c.** Reconcile only records tags *without* git-board's provenance mark (§4). Tags git-board cut are owned by their cut, which removes the race for every new tag. It needs a \"resume this cut\" path for interrupted ones.\n\n\n**Decided.** A pushed tag is never deleted (git-tag(1), \"On Re-tagging\"). So cut, promote and rollback check everything first, and refuse:\n- when offline;\n- dev-only apps (the followed environment is the last one);\n- a commit that doesn't contain what runs in the last environment (unless hotfix);\n- a final with no candidate that ran in the environment before last (unless hotfix);\n- a yanked release;\n- an image in no registry: promote and rollback never build;\n- when a deploy is asked, an environment that isn't adopted or wouldn't move forward. Hotfix is exempt only for an environment before the last.\n\n**Why.** Otherwise a step that fails after the tag leaves a published tag on a commit that may never run.\n\n**Cost:**\n- The checks read state that can change before the push, for instance another clone deploys meanwhile. The deploy can still refuse after the tag. That is rarer now, but not impossible.\n- Offline refusal means no cut on a plane, even with `--no-deploy`.\n\n**To settle:**\n- Should an offline cut be allowed, with the tag and record queued for the next push? \"Decentralized first\" argues yes; \"origin decides who pushed first\" argues no.\n- Is \"dev-only apps never tag\" right for single-environment apps like fonts (prod only, main → prod)? They would only ever use `deploy`. The refusal message says \"dev-only\", and should say \"single-environment\".\n\n\n**Decided.** git-board cuts annotated tags with the message `\u003ctag\u003e (git-board)`. `ReleaseRow.beforeGitBoard` is true when a tag's subject lacks that suffix, and the UI labels such rows \"before git-board\".\n\n**Why.** The record's author can't tell, because Reconcile records old tags under whoever ran it. The tag carries its provenance in git itself (`git tag -n`).\n\n**Cost:**\n- It's a message convention: a hand-made tag with that suffix reads as git-board's.\n- Changing the mark later makes old tags misread.\n\n**Options:**\n- a trailer, `Released-by: git-board` (more conventional);\n- a record field, `origin: cut | reconcile`;\n- signed tags. v1 is \"attributed, not signed\".\n\n\n- **What it is.** `enac-it-harbor.epfl.ch/epfl-enac` is a private registry with robot credentials; the robot name contains a `$`. It's for images that must stay on EPFL infrastructure, such as licensed fonts, so never ghcr.\n- **It fits the model.** It's a `[release] deploy` target whose registry is Harbor. An image is only copied to registries whose overlay lists it, so fonts would never touch ghcr. Laptops need a Harbor login: a robot account or a personal CLI secret.\n- **fonts would be configured as:** `environments = [\"prod\"]`, `follow = \"prod\"`, `[branches] base = \"main\"`. `git-board deploy prod` works, and `release` refuses (see §3).\n- **Harbor tag immutability** would refuse two things git-board does today: re-pushing a chart at the same version (dev re-deploys) and re-pushing the scratch `:scan-\u003csha\u003e` tag. Harbor's own Trivy scan, which can block pulls of vulnerable images, could complement the laptop scan. Its retention rules could clean `scan-*` tags.\n- **The GitLab component's `registries` JSON** (`prefix`, `username_var`, `password_var`, `argo_repositories`) maps directly onto `[release] deploy` (`repo` + `registry`).","files":null}]}